ets the need: add the exact hosts with `add_allowed_hosts`, do not add hosts or variables speculatively, and prefer exact hosts over `network_access` \"unrestricted\". NEVER put secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords) in environment variables or the setup script: both are stored in plain text on the environment and shown in the approval prompt; keys go through request_credential, if you have it. Copy `name` and `applies_to` from get_environment into `environment_name` and `applies_to` exactly: the person approving sees them, and the call is rejected if they do not match, because an edit changes a shared, stored environment, not just this session. Changes are saved to the environment right away. In this session, host and network changes take effect within seconds (about 30 at most). Variables and the MCP-server setting apply the next time the session starts or resumes. The setup script runs the next time a container is built fresh, not on a resume. This tool can also remove exact allowlist entries (`remove_allowed_hosts`), change the network access level (`network_access`: \"limited\" or \"unrestricted\") and, for limited access, whether package registries and MCP server hosts are reachable without listing them, and rename or re-describe the environment (`new_name`, `new_description`). `setup_script` replaces the environment's setup script (`script_change` removes it); on an organization environment it runs in everyone's sessions on that environment. If this session's repositories needed set-up here (dependencies installed, a long build) and get_environment says `has_setup_script` is false, and its description puts the change here, add one with `setup_script` and `script_change` \"adds a setup script\" without waiting to be asked, once the set-up has worked. For an organization environment, say in your message that the change, a script included, applies to everyone's sessions on it. If the user declines, do not propose a change for that need again in this session, with